{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-20c33718cb274b2095a3a3a8bf23e482","ordinal":561,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.61","text":"\n\nAdding one cup at a time in this way and reasoning similarly, one can conclude that the twenty cups of mixture we see ought to be twenty thousand cups and more, as far as the hypothesis about the manner of blending goes, and that the same cups are also only two. An incongruity could hardly go further than that. So this hypothesis about blending is absurd too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c504fe7d46644418a2e07961c923d425","ordinal":562,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.62","text":"\n\nSo blending cannot come about when the substances alone are mixed with one another, nor when the qualities alone are, nor when both are, nor when neither is; and nothing besides these can be conceived. Therefore the manner of blending, and of mixing in general, is inconceivable. So the so-called elements can produce compounds neither by being placed side by side in contact nor by being blended or mixed. If that is so, the dogmatists’ account of nature is inconceivable, as far as this argument goes too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-69a7a853d76f4bdbbb7a8e2c620c30dd","ordinal":563,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.63","text":"\n\nIn addition to what has been said, one could consider the argument about motions, and how the dogmatists’ account of nature would be thought impossible on that score too. For compounds must certainly come about through some motion of the elements and of the active principle. So if we point out that no kind of motion is agreed upon, it will be clear that, even if everything above is granted by hypothesis, the dogmatists have worked through their so-called physics in vain."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e61c940ffad54bc892abb0e4e9d1a515","ordinal":564,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.64","text":"\n\nThose who seem to have treated motion most thoroughly say that it has six kinds: change of place, natural alteration, growth, diminution, generation and destruction. We shall consider each of these kinds of motion separately, beginning with change of place. According to the dogmatists, this is the motion by which the moving thing passes from place to place, either as a whole or by parts. As a whole is the case of people walking; by parts is the case of a sphere rotating around its center, for while the whole sphere stays in the same place, its parts exchange places."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f3ea28fa50b24d00aac8e174f386b2ef","ordinal":565,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.65","text":"\n\nThere have been, I think, three main positions about motion. Ordinary life and some philosophers hold that there is motion. Parmenides, Melissus and certain others hold that there is not. And the skeptics have said that there is “no more” motion than not: as far as appearances go, there seems to be motion, but as far as philosophical argument goes, it is not real. So we shall set out the case on both sides, from those who hold that there is motion and from those who declare that there is none. If we find the disagreement equipollent, we shall be compelled to say that there is “no more” motion than not, as far as what is said goes."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5889174a1545416f97b7cadd7fdcd422","ordinal":566,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.66","text":"\n\nLet us begin with those who say that it is real. These rely above all on clarity. If there is no motion, they say, how does the sun travel from east to west? How does it produce the seasons of the year, which come about as it approaches us and withdraws from us? How do ships put out from one harbor and come in to others very far from the first? And how does the man who abolishes motion go out of his house and come back again? These things, they say, are utterly undeniable. That is why one of the Cynics, when presented with the argument against motion, gave no answer but stood up and walked about, showing by his action and through clarity that motion is real. This, then, is how they try to shame those who hold the opposite position;"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-06d3573baa0c450bb3f7ec09df79d3d3","ordinal":567,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.67","text":"\n\nwhile those who abolish the existence of motion argue in ways like the following. If something moves, it is moved either by itself or by something else. But if by something else, ⟨.........⟩. For what is said to be moved by something else will be moved either without a cause or because of some cause. Nothing, they say, comes about without a cause. And if it is moved because of some cause, the cause because of which it is moved will be what moves it; and so there is a fall into infinite regress, following the line of thought stated a little earlier."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1cebb5058df94cfd86c05300730921c9","ordinal":568,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.68","text":"\n\nBesides, if what moves something is active, and what is active is in motion, it too will need another mover, and the second a third, and so on to infinity, so that the motion turns out to have no beginning, which is absurd. So not everything that moves is moved by something else. Nor is it moved by itself. ⟨For⟩ everything that moves something moves it by pushing it forward, or by pulling it, or by pushing it up, or by pressing it down. So what moves itself will have to move itself in one of these ways."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1eab499287f64dbaa69c68416e208f0d","ordinal":569,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.69","text":"\n\nBut if it moves itself by pushing, it will be behind itself; if by pulling, in front of itself; if by pushing up, beneath itself; if by pressing down, above itself. But it is impossible for anything to be above, in front of, beneath or behind itself; so it is impossible for anything to be moved by itself. And if nothing is moved either by itself or by something else, nothing is moved at all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c74585392eba4a4caf39dcc6affcb3a5","ordinal":570,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.70","text":"\n\nAnd if someone takes refuge in impulse and choice, we must remind him of the disagreement about what is up to us, and that it has remained undecidable, since we have not found a criterion of truth so far."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-045de7bb2cc946b58af5661a94d69901","ordinal":571,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.71","text":"\n\nThis too should be said. If something moves, it moves either in the place where it is or in the place where it is not. But not in the place where it is, for it stays in it, if it is in it. Nor in the place where it is not, for where a thing is not, it can neither act nor be acted upon. So nothing moves. This argument is Diodorus Cronus’, and it has met with many rejoinders. Because of the character of this treatise, we shall set out the more striking of them, along with the verdict on them that appears to us."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2354a228110141c1aee743345981c8b8","ordinal":572,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.72","text":"\n\nSome, then, say that a thing can move in the place where it is. At any rate, they say, spheres spinning around their centers move while staying in the same place. Against them we should transfer the argument to each of the sphere’s parts. We point out that, as far as the argument goes, the sphere does not move even by parts, and so conclude that nothing moves in the place where it is either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b1c7f8e9118b4e61a8c5f41876552f34","ordinal":573,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.73","text":"\n\nWe shall do the same against those who say that what moves occupies two places, the one in which it is and the one into which it travels. We shall ask them when the moving thing travels from the place in which it is into the other. Is it when it is in the first place or when it is in the second? When it is in the first place, it is not passing into the second, for it is still in the first. And when it is not in the first, it is not passing from it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-43700164b89f485c86c1597a7be66e8c","ordinal":574,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.74","text":"\n\nBesides, the point at issue is being taken for granted. A thing cannot be active where it is not; and surely no one who does not grant outright that a thing moves will grant that it travels into some place."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0a83f0841efc40f28458f61ef5d29f8d","ordinal":575,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.75","text":"\n\nSome, however, also say this. “Place” is used in two ways: broadly, as when my house is my place, and strictly, as for example the air that has molded itself around the surface of my body. What moves, then, is said to move in a place, not in the strict place but in the broad one. Against them we can subdivide the broad place. The body said to move is, strictly speaking, in one part of it, its strict place, and is not in the others, the remaining parts of the broad place. Then, having concluded that a thing can move neither in the place where it is nor in the place where it is not, we can infer that nothing can move in the broad place either, which is called a place only loosely. For the broad place is made up of the place where the thing strictly is and the place where it strictly is not, and it has been shown that a thing can move in neither."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b5ca1026e1b64fc7b057c5557cd89fe4","ordinal":576,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.76","text":"\n\nOne should also put forward the following argument. If something moves, it moves either step ⟨by step⟩ or over a divisible interval all at once. But nothing can move either step by step or over a divisible interval all at once, as we shall show; so nothing moves. That nothing can move step by step is plain straight off. Suppose bodies, and the places and times in which bodies are said to move, are divided to infinity. Then there will be no motion, since among infinitely many things it is impossible to find a first one, from which the thing said to move will first move."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-91ff43ad35fb40f5ac6ecea830215af3","ordinal":577,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.77","text":"\n\nAnd suppose the things mentioned end in something partless, and each moving thing likewise passes through the first partless bit of place in the first partless bit of its time. Then all moving things are equally fast, the swiftest horse and the tortoise, for instance, which is even more absurd than the first alternative. So motion does not take place step by step. Nor does it take place over a divisible interval all at once."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-da38e501e3704899b1c81a46d65f1dc9","ordinal":578,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.78","text":"\n\nAs they say, non-evident things must be attested by appearances. Now anyone who is to cover the length of a stade must first cover the first part of the stade, then the second, and the others likewise. In the same way, everything that moves should move step by step. For suppose the moving thing were said to pass all at once through all the parts of the place in which it is said to move. Then it will be in all those parts at the same time. And if one part of the place it moves through were cold and another hot, or, say, one black and another white, so that they could color what came into contact with them, the moving thing would be hot and cold, black and white, all at once, which is absurd."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7cf674cffc2149af8998b9ba72760a76","ordinal":579,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.79","text":"\n\nNext, let them tell us how much place the moving thing passes through all at once. If they say it is unlimited, they will be accepting that something moves through the whole earth all at once. If they shun this, let them define the size of the place for us. They might try to define precisely the place beyond which the moving thing cannot pass all at once, by even a hair’s breadth. But that is arbitrary and rash, perhaps even laughable; and besides, it falls into the original difficulty. Everything will be equally fast, if each of them likewise makes the transitions of its movements by precisely defined places."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6a8fce26058f4d07a3ccc2b5d121bd24","ordinal":580,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.80","text":"\n\nAnd suppose they say that the moving thing moves all at once through a place that is small, but not precisely defined. Then we shall be able, in the manner of the sorites puzzle, always to add a minute extent of place to the size proposed. If they stop somewhere while we are putting this series of questions, they will fall back into precise definition and that piece of fantasy. If they admit the increase, we shall force them to grant that something can move all at once across the extent of the whole earth. So the things said to move do not move over a divisible interval all at once either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0a539016fb514bf49144966b48941ab4","ordinal":581,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.81","text":"\n\nAnd if nothing moves either over a divisible place all at once or step ⟨by step⟩, nothing moves at all. This, and more besides, is what those who abolish motion from place to place say. As for us, we are unable to overturn either these arguments or the appearance they follow when they introduce the existence of motion. So, as far as the opposition between appearances and arguments goes, we suspend judgment about whether there is motion or not."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7b49818dc7ad4e1396940e2f6831f343","ordinal":582,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.82","text":"\n\nUsing the same reasoning, we also suspend judgment about growth and diminution. Clarity seems to introduce their existence, and the arguments seem to overturn it. Just consider: what grows must exist and be real and increase in size, so that if someone said that one thing had grown when an addition was made to another, he will be speaking falsely. Now substance never stands still; it is always flowing, one substance coming in to replace another. So what is said to have grown does not have its earlier substance plus another added to it, but an entirely different one."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8780a75a176b492a91cf69cf00a3cc77","ordinal":583,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.83","text":"\n\nSuppose, for example, there is a piece of wood three cubits long, and someone brings another, ten cubits long, and says that he has made the three-cubit piece grow. He will be speaking falsely, because the one is entirely different from the other. So too in the case of everything said to grow: the earlier matter flows away and other matter comes in, and if what is said to be added is added, no one would call such a thing growth, but total alteration."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-584d3ef8ac6e4a7580bf269a63a59e80","ordinal":584,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.84","text":"\n\nThe same argument applies to diminution too. How could what is not real at all be said to have diminished? Besides, diminution comes about by subtraction and growth by addition. So if there is no such thing as subtraction or addition, then neither diminution nor growth is anything either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-dd27101ddd014bbdb9f6a02ee370c30d","ordinal":585,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.85","text":"\n\nThat subtraction is nothing they infer as follows. If something is subtracted from something, either the equal is subtracted from the equal, or the greater from the lesser, or the lesser from the greater. But subtraction takes place in none of these ways, as we shall establish; so subtraction is impossible. That it takes place in none of the ways mentioned is plain from what follows. What is subtracted from something must, before the subtraction, be contained in what it is subtracted from."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-cf17802ce9924e108f1c1fd45df087a1","ordinal":586,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.86","text":"\n\nBut the equal is not contained in the equal, six in six, for example. The container must be greater than what it contains, and what something is subtracted from must be greater than what is subtracted, so that something is left over after the subtraction; that seems to be what distinguishes subtraction from total removal. Nor is the greater contained in the lesser, six ⟨in⟩ five, for example; that is incongruous."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1b0e92a2421748a884514bf5f96a3f19","ordinal":587,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.87","text":"\n\nAnd for this reason the lesser is not contained in the greater either. If five is contained in six, as the lesser in the greater, four will be contained in five, three in four, two in three, and one in two. So six will contain five, four, three, two and one. Added together these make the number fifteen, which, once it is granted that the lesser is contained in the greater, turns out to be contained in six. In the same way, the number thirty-five is contained in the fifteen that is contained in the six, and so on down, until there are infinitely many. But it is absurd to say that infinitely many numbers are contained in the number six; so it is also absurd to say that the lesser is contained in the greater."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-357386ee6bb64b269556c0e010c9bcd4","ordinal":588,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.88","text":"\n\nSo what is subtracted from something must be contained in what it is to be subtracted from. But the equal is not contained in the equal, the greater is not contained in the lesser, and the lesser is not contained in the greater. If all this holds, nothing is subtracted from anything. Again, if something is subtracted from something, either a whole is subtracted from a whole, or a part from a part, or a whole from a part, or a part from a whole."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c8c3d10e0b4f4b07bad8644c5c61e039","ordinal":589,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.89","text":"\n\nTo say that a whole is subtracted, whether from a whole or from a part, is plainly incoherent. So it remains to say that a part is subtracted, either from a whole or from a part, and this is absurd. Take an example, resting the argument on numbers for clarity’s sake. Let there be a ten, and let a unit be said to be subtracted from it. This unit can be subtracted neither from the whole ten nor from the part of the ten that remains, that is, the nine, as I shall show. So it is not subtracted at all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-31b8ccb0939f4a26850e46e81c5a65ff","ordinal":590,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.90","text":"\n\nFor suppose the unit is subtracted from the whole ten. The ten is neither something other than the ten units nor any one of the units, but the aggregate of all the units. So if the unit is to be subtracted from the whole ten, it has to be subtracted from each unit. In the first place, nothing can be subtracted from a unit at all, since units are indivisible. For this reason the unit will not be subtracted from the ten in this way."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7bfa9f8f64354d0e9fe27d5b93e6e0ae","ordinal":591,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.91","text":"\n\nBut even if someone were to grant that the unit is subtracted from each of the units, the unit will have ten parts, and having ten parts it will be a ten. What is more, ten other parts are left over, from which the ten parts of the so-called unit were subtracted, so the ten will be twenty. But it is absurd to say that one is ten and ten is twenty, and that what is indivisible by their own account is divided. So it is absurd to say that the unit is subtracted from the whole ten."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-bd87f3fe9d18459c834ca81df779aefe","ordinal":592,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.92","text":"\n\nNor is the unit subtracted from the nine that remains. For what something is subtracted from does not remain intact, but the nine remains intact after that unit has been subtracted. Besides, the nine is nothing apart from the nine units. So if the unit were said to be subtracted from the whole of it, there will be a subtraction of nine. If it is said to be subtracted from a part of the nine, then either it is subtracted from the eight, and the same absurdities will follow, or it is subtracted from the last unit, and they will be saying that the unit is divisible, which is absurd."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d999eec6487840d48c8adeefe2ccf7fb","ordinal":593,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.93","text":"\n\nSo the unit is not subtracted from the nine either. And if it is subtracted neither from the whole ten nor from a part of it, then a part cannot be subtracted from a whole or from a part. If, then, nothing is subtracted as a whole from a whole, or as a part from a whole, or as a whole from a part, or as a part from a part, then nothing is subtracted from anything."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a488cb4b56e2449886e994e7033d09c6","ordinal":594,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.94","text":"\n\nAddition, too, has been taken by them to be one of the impossible things. For what is added, they say, is added either to itself, or to what was there before, or to the compound of both. None of these holds up, so nothing is added to anything. For instance, let there be a quantity of four cups, and let one cup be added. I ask what it is added to. It cannot be added to itself, since what is added is other than what it is added to, and nothing is other than itself."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-04004400ad5f490a832ac188bb5809a7","ordinal":595,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.95","text":"\n\nNor can it be added to the compound of both, the four cups and the cup, for how could anything be added to what does not yet exist? Besides, if the added cup is mixed with the four cups and the cup, there will be a quantity of six cups, made up of the four cups, the cup, and the added cup."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-33fb625d2dc0405088440e55449b13f7","ordinal":596,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.96","text":"\n\nAnd suppose the cup is added to the four cups alone. What is extended alongside something is equal to what it is extended alongside, so the cup, extended alongside the quantity of four cups, will double the four cups, and the whole quantity will come to eight cups. That is not what we observe. If, then, what is said to be added is added neither to itself, nor to what was there before, nor to the compound of these, and there is nothing besides these, then nothing is added to anything."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-36983b04bab342b4901462c9f63383ae","ordinal":597,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.97","text":"\n\nAlong with the reality of addition, subtraction and local motion, transposition is canceled too (for it is subtraction from one thing and addition to another, by way of transfer),"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9ebab4f7d50149efbf46386736a93dde","ordinal":598,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.98","text":"\n\nand so are whole and part. For a whole seems to come about through the coming together and addition of parts, and to stop being a whole through the subtraction of some part or parts. Besides, if there is a whole, either it is something other than its parts, or its parts themselves are ⟨the⟩ whole."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-72ae05a7aa6446249d3e42110f066072","ordinal":599,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.99","text":"\n\nNow the whole does not appear to be anything other than the parts. At any rate, when the parts are removed, nothing is left over that would let us reckon the whole as something other than them. And if the parts themselves are the whole, the whole will be merely a name and an empty label, with no reality of its own. In the same way a distance is nothing apart from the things that stand apart, and a framework of beams is nothing apart from the beams laid on. So there is no whole."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5a77d2dc6dbe4b05b526f63950f60639","ordinal":600,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.100","text":"\n\nNor are there parts. For if there are parts, they are parts either of the whole, or of one another, or each of itself. They are not parts of the whole, since there is nothing besides the parts (and in any case, on that view the parts would be parts of themselves, since each of the parts is said to help make up the whole). Nor are they parts of one another, since a part seems to be contained in what it is a part of, and it is absurd to say that the hand, for example, is contained in the foot."}]}